46
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:03,020
One of the things that
you'll notice if you look at

47
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:04,955
this code and if
you try to run it,

48
00:02:04,955 --> 00:02:07,880
if you run it with no file,

49
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:11,630
the application's going
to try to operate on

50
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:13,220
this empty file based on

51
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:16,735
the parameters passed in
the run empty file script.

52
00:02:16,735 --> 00:02:18,935
One of the things
you'll notice is,

53
00:02:18,935 --> 00:02:22,010
despite the fact that I'm
opening it here with read,

54
00:02:22,010 --> 00:02:24,485
write, and execute
permissions for others.

55
00:02:24,485 --> 00:02:27,620
If you look at the
empty file permissions

56
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:31,190
for the file that gets
created in Open Stack,

57
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:35,220
on your virtual-box VM,

58
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:36,585
or on your Linux machine,

59
00:02:36,585 --> 00:02:38,015
what you'll notice is that

60
00:02:38,015 --> 00:02:39,290
the write permission is

61
00:02:39,290 --> 00:02:41,420
actually missing for
the others group,

62
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:42,860
even though we've specified it

63
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:45,425
here in the open permissions.

64
00:02:45,425 --> 00:02:47,910
What happened to that,
right permission?

65
00:02:47,910 --> 00:02:50,535
It was actually
cleared by the umask.

66
00:02:50,535 --> 00:02:53,730
That begs the question,
what is umask?

67
00:02:53,730 --> 00:02:56,255
Umask is really just
a per-process way

68
00:02:56,255 --> 00:02:59,300
that you can restrict the
permission set on files.

69
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:03,770
If you can basically
make it so that even if

70
00:03:03,770 --> 00:03:05,360
an application is trying to open

71
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:08,675
a file with some
set of permissions,

72
00:03:08,675 --> 00:03:11,405
as a system administrator,

73
00:03:11,405 --> 00:03:12,830
restrict the level of

74
00:03:12,830 --> 00:03:14,960
permissions that a
person could give by

75
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:19,220
default from that process on
any files that were created.

76
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:21,770
There are reasons why
you might not want

77
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:24,050
a default application to be

78
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:26,675
creating files that
anyone could write.

79
00:03:26,675 --> 00:03:30,720
By having the default
umask set to 0002,

80
00:03:30,720 --> 00:03:35,925
what this essentially does
is a bitwise invert or

81
00:03:35,925 --> 00:03:38,745
a bitwise mask off of the bit

82
00:03:38,745 --> 00:03:42,000
two which of course would
be the write permission.

83
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,195
This is a way that you
can set four processes.

84
00:03:45,195 --> 00:03:50,879
Don't give new file
creation permission

85
00:03:50,879 --> 00:03:53,530
for right by default
using umask 2,

86
00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:56,165
and that happens to be
the default for Ubuntu,

87
00:03:56,165 --> 00:03:59,020
that's why we're seeing
this particular behavior.

88
00:03:59,020 --> 00:04:02,250
What would happen if I
change my umask to three?

89
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:04,565
Then instead of just
masking off the right bit,

90
00:04:04,565 --> 00:04:06,410
I'm also going to
mask off execute bit.

91
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:07,760
If you can use

92
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:10,040
the umask command to do
this, and when you do,

93
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:11,750
you'll see that creating that

94
00:04:11,750 --> 00:04:14,510
empty file results in
neither the right nor the

95
00:04:14,510 --> 00:04:16,445
execute bit set clear

96
00:04:16,445 --> 00:04:20,040
or neither right nor
execute bit itself.

97
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:22,580
One other thing I wanted

98
00:04:22,580 --> 00:04:24,110
to introduce here now
that we're talking

99
00:04:24,110 --> 00:04:25,940
about little more about

100
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:29,045
