kernel space and userspace.

101
00:04:29,045 --> 00:04:31,340
I want to introduce
the tool Strace,

102
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,110
and this is a really useful tool

103
00:04:33,110 --> 00:04:35,090
that we'll be using throughout
the semester because it

104
00:04:35,090 --> 00:04:36,770
helps us see exactly
what's happening

105
00:04:36,770 --> 00:04:39,235
between user space
and kernel space.

106
00:04:39,235 --> 00:04:41,795
It's like a protocol
analyzer for

107
00:04:41,795 --> 00:04:43,190
the system call traces that are

108
00:04:43,190 --> 00:04:44,690
happening from userspace,

109
00:04:44,690 --> 00:04:46,550
showing exactly what's getting

110
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:49,210
called and what's getting
returned from the kernel.

111
00:04:49,210 --> 00:04:51,930
If you run the Strace with

112
00:04:51,930 --> 00:04:54,440
the read-file execution
application that I

113
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:57,970
mentioned earlier and the
parameter empty file,

114
00:04:57,970 --> 00:05:02,330
you can see that the open
command, so open app,

115
00:05:02,330 --> 00:05:04,070
in this case, the
actual system call for

116
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:06,635
open is occurring and
it's returning three.

117
00:05:06,635 --> 00:05:08,300
Three is going to be our file

118
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:10,110
descriptor and then you can see

119
00:05:10,110 --> 00:05:14,630
the call that's going to check
that the file for content,

120
00:05:14,630 --> 00:05:15,980
there's a read on

121
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:18,110
that same three file
descriptor that

122
00:05:18,110 --> 00:05:20,585
we opened here in
the previous step,

123
00:05:20,585 --> 00:05:22,990
and you can see that it's
returning zero bytes.

124
00:05:22,990 --> 00:05:25,250
One thing to know
about this is this

125
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:27,475
did not require any source
code for read file.

126
00:05:27,475 --> 00:05:30,410
This could work on
anyone, any application,

127
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:32,345
including just a binary that you

128
00:05:32,345 --> 00:05:35,210
got from a closed source
application for instance.

129
00:05:35,210 --> 00:05:38,060
Again, it's a really useful tool

130
00:05:38,060 --> 00:05:40,040
that we'll use throughout
the semester and

131
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:42,060
it can help illustrate

132
00:05:42,060 --> 00:05:43,180
how the interaction is

133
00:05:43,180 --> 00:05:45,155
working between user
and kernel space.

134
00:05:45,155 --> 00:05:48,050
In addition to file data,

135
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:50,420
we can also use the
open command with.

136
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:52,790
With fifos or pipes,

137
00:05:52,790 --> 00:05:54,590
and fifos and pipes have

138
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:56,915
additional option of 0_NONBLOCK,

139
00:05:56,915 --> 00:06:00,575
which can be used to open
in non-blocking mode.

140
00:06:00,575 --> 00:06:03,440
When you use in
non-blocking mode,

141
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:04,850
rather than the default behavior

142
00:06:04,850 --> 00:06:07,700
for fifo or a pipe,
which is the block,

143
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:11,600
waiting for access or waiting
for data from the pipe,

144
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,800
you'll get a return of
EAGAIN to mean that there's

145
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:17,840
no data available which
you can then pull from

146
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:21,360
your application to wait
for data available.

147
00:06:21,550 --> 00:06:24,185
There's an example of this,

148
00:06:24,185 --> 00:06:28,685
creating a pipe and using
this with readfile.

149
00:06:28,685 --> 00:06:35,165
It's also attached
to this slide deck.

150
00:06:35,165 --> 00:06:39,155
What you can see if
you run this example,

151
00:06:39,155 --> 00:06:40,850
if you start one thread,

152
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:42,290
you'll see that by
default it's going to

153
00:06:42,290 --> 00:06:43,820
block waiting for access

154
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:45,620
to the pipe that's

155
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:47,675
created in the current
directory called pipe.

156
00:06:47,675 --> 00:06:49,370
If you open another
terminal window

157
00:06:49,370 --> 00:06:50,600
and you just echo anything,

158
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:51,995
write anything into that pipe,

159
00:06:51,995 --> 00:06:53,795
you'll get the response and

160
00:06:53,795 --> 00:06:55,970
the application will come

161
00:06:55,970 --> 00:06:58,265
back with the byte that
was written into the pipe.

162
00:06:58,265 --> 00:06:59,930
That's the default
behavior that you

163
00:06:59,930 --> 00:07:02,085
get without specifying
0_NONBLOCK.

164
00:07:02,085 --> 00:07:04,375
But if you specify 0_NONBLOCK,

165
00:07:04,375 --> 00:07:07,980
what you'll see when
you run this example

166
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:10,220
is that instead of
blocking and waiting for

167
00:07:10,220 --> 00:07:12,410
this command to be executed
from the terminal,

168
00:07:12,410 --> 00:07:14,690
you'll get a EAGAIN
returned back from read,

169
00:07:14,690 --> 00:07:17,720
indicating that there's no
data available to read,

170
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:21,900
and you can just call read
as many times as you want.

171
00:07:22,780 --> 00:07:25,730
We've talked about
reading files and some of

172
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:28,505
the modes related
to read access.

173
00:07:28,505 --> 00:07:30,755
You can of course
also write a file.

174
00:07:30,755 --> 00:07:32,945
This again is going to
look really similar to

175
00:07:32,945 --> 00:07:35,330
what you do with f
write where you're

176
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:37,715
going to specify the
file descriptor and

177
00:07:37,715 --> 00:07:40,445
then give it a buffer and
the size of bytes to write,

178
00:07:40,445 --> 00:07:43,385
and it's going to return the
number of bytes written.

179
00:07:43,385 --> 00:07:45,620
One thing to know, and I've

180
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:47,690
mentioned before that
there are options for

181
00:07:47,690 --> 00:07:52,340
synchronized operation on
the mode option for open.

182
00:07:52,340 --> 00:07:54,620
So synchronized IO becomes

183
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:57,410
important when we talk
about write behavior.

184
00:07:57,410 --> 00:08:01,100
By default, if

185
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:02,390
you write to a file and

186
00:08:02,390 --> 00:08:04,520
that file is ultimately
located on disk,

187
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:06,395
one thing that might
be a little confusing

188
00:08:06,395 --> 00:08:07,670
is that when you complete

189
00:08:07,670 --> 00:08:09,020
the write command and let's say

190
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:10,805
the write command
completes successfully,

191
00:08:10,805 --> 00:08:12,260
that does not actually mean that

192
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:14,540
the bits were
manipulated on disk.

193
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:16,340
What that just means is
that you've written it

194
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:18,110
into a buffer somewhere in

195
00:08:18,110 --> 00:08:19,970
the kernel and it's intended to

196
00:08:19,970 --> 00:08:22,650
be destined for
the disk ultimate.

197
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:24,205
Why would we do it this way?

198
00:08:24,205 --> 00:08:26,110
Why wouldn't you wait until

199
00:08:26,110 --> 00:08:28,600
the write returns
with success and

200
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:29,950
actually is written
completely out to

201
00:08:29,950 --> 00:08:33,275
the disk in order to know
that that was successful?

202
00:08:33,275 --> 00:08:34,580
Well, the main reason for that

203
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:35,915
is because disks are slow.

204
00:08:35,915 --> 00:08:38,120
Relative your program seek

205
00:08:38,120 --> 00:08:40,430
times on a spinning hard
drive, for instance,

206
00:08:40,430 --> 00:08:42,800
are in probably tens to
hundreds of milliseconds,

207
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:44,540
which is an eternity
when you're talking

208
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:49,610
about code running on
a gigahertz processor.

209
00:08:49,610 --> 00:08:53,060
That's really the main reason
that by default you won't

210
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:57,005
have writes waiting for
data to be written to disk.

211
00:08:57,005 --> 00:08:59,630
But we can control this
when we're opening based

212
00:08:59,630 --> 00:09:03,120
on how we flag for
synchronized IO.

213
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:06,530
If we don't use synchronized IO,

214
00:09:06,530 --> 00:09:07,880
which again is the default,

215
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,130
when would it be a problem
that we hadn't actually

216
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:12,680
flushed all the file
contents of disc?

217
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:14,825
This would be a problem
on unclean shut down

218
00:09:14,825 --> 00:09:16,850
because we might have
an application that

219
00:09:16,850 --> 00:09:20,975
thinks that it wrote successfully
some output to a file,

220
00:09:20,975 --> 00:09:23,720
maybe updated some
other thread or

221
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:25,250
some other application
or wrote something

222
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:26,975
out to the Internet that said,

223
00:09:26,975 --> 00:09:29,750
some action was performed
on its local file system.

224
00:09:29,750 --> 00:09:31,655
But then let's say
we lose power,

225
00:09:31,655 --> 00:09:34,460
and we lost power
before the kernel had

226
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:35,870
a chance to write
the content and

227
00:09:35,870 --> 00:09:37,565
it's buffers out to disk.

228
00:09:37,565 --> 00:09:39,590
Well, in that case, there'll

229
00:09:39,590 --> 00:09:41,120
be a mismatch between whoever

230
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:42,500
was notified about the state of

231
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:44,750
the file on the
local file system,

232
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:46,565
and actual state that's

233
00:09:46,565 --> 00:09:49,190
going to be on the disk
the next time you boot up.

234
00:09:49,190 --> 00:09:50,915
How can we manage this risk?

235
00:09:50,915 --> 00:09:52,100
We really have to
know about it as

236
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:54,020
application programmers
and we have to

237
00:09:54,020 --> 00:09:55,370
know that we need to do

238
00:09:55,370 --> 00:09:58,355
a synchronization on specific
steps where it's important.

239
00:09:58,355 --> 00:10:02,000
There's different ways
to do synchronization.

240
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,310
There's fsync,
there's fdatasync,

241
00:10:04,310 --> 00:10:05,690
and there's also 0_SYNC,

242
00:10:05,690 --> 00:10:06,770
which is the option
that you could

243
00:10:06,770 --> 00:10:08,405
pass into the open command.

244
00:10:08,405 --> 00:10:09,920
There's also an
option where you can

245
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:11,315
configure disk write caching

246
00:10:11,315 --> 00:10:14,660
to set the rules
about when a write

247
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:17,630
from disk is reported
as successful relative

248
00:10:17,630 --> 00:10:19,010
to when it was actually

249
00:10:19,010 --> 00:10:21,380
physically written
to the medium disk.

250
00:10:21,380 --> 00:10:24,740
The Linux system programming
Chapter 2 also talks

251
00:10:24,740 --> 00:10:27,065
about the sync command
as one way to do this.

252
00:10:27,065 --> 00:10:29,150
But it mentions that you
typically don't want to

253
00:10:29,150 --> 00:10:31,370
use this for
performance reasons.

254
00:10:31,370 --> 00:10:33,335
The reason is where fsync and

255
00:10:33,335 --> 00:10:35,630
fdatasync are going to
work on a file descriptor,

256
00:10:35,630 --> 00:10:37,280
sync is just going
to work globally and

257
00:10:37,280 --> 00:10:39,095
tell the OS to
synchronize everything.

258
00:10:39,095 --> 00:10:41,615
What that means is that
any application that's

259
00:10:41,615 --> 00:10:44,540
writing to a file descriptor
that is not synchronized,

260
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:47,000
it's going to try to synchronize
all those files at once.

261
